pg_bp_lru -- Design Notes
==========================

This document describes the internal design of pg_bp_lru for engineers
who need to understand, modify, or debug the implementation.


A. Algorithm
------------

LRU maintains a single doubly-linked list of all cached pages, ordered
from least recently used (head) to most recently used (tail).

Operations:

    On hit:       Remove from current position, insert at MRU (tail).
    On miss:      No-op.  Only a stat counter is incremented.
    On evict:     Remove from the list.
    On new tag:   Insert at MRU (tail).
    Get victim:   Scan from LRU (head) for the first unpinned buffer.

There are no ghost lists.  There is no adaptation parameter.  There is
no frequency tracking.  This is the simplest possible implementation of
a cache replacement algorithm.

The miss path does not touch the list at all -- it only increments
an atomic counter.  This means misses are effectively lock-free.


B. Implementation
-----------------

Data structures
...............

LruNode (one per buffer):

    int   prev;      -- index of previous node (-1 = none)
    int   next;      -- index of next node (-1 = none)
    bool  on_list;   -- true if this node is on the LRU list

LruControl (one per pool):

    slock_t            lru_lock;        -- spinlock protecting the list
    int                nbuffers;        -- number of physical buffers
    int                first_buf_id;    -- global buffer ID of first buffer
    int                list_head;       -- LRU end (-1 = empty)
    int                list_tail;       -- MRU end (-1 = empty)
    int                list_size;       -- number of buffers on the list
    pg_atomic_uint64   stat_hits;       -- total cache hits
    pg_atomic_uint64   stat_misses;     -- total cache misses
    pg_atomic_uint64   stat_evictions;  -- total evictions

Memory layout
.............

All state lives in a single DSM allocation:

    [ LruControl (MAXALIGN'd) ][ LruNode[0] ... LruNode[nbuffers-1] ]

The LRU_NODES(ctl) macro returns a pointer to the start of the node
array by advancing past the MAXALIGN'd LruControl header.

Memory overhead per buffer is approximately 12 bytes (two 4-byte ints
for prev/next plus a 1-byte bool, padded to 4-byte alignment).  This
is the lowest per-buffer overhead of any replacement algorithm in the
buffer pool framework.

Total shared memory: MAXALIGN(sizeof(LruControl)) + nbuffers * 12.

List operations
...............

lru_list_remove(ctl, nodes, idx):
    Unlinks node[idx] from the doubly-linked list.  Updates head/tail
    pointers as needed.  Decrements list_size.  Caller must hold
    lru_lock.

lru_mru_insert(ctl, nodes, idx):
    Inserts node[idx] at the MRU (tail) end.  Updates head pointer if
    the list was empty.  Increments list_size.  Caller must hold
    lru_lock.

Both operations are O(1).

Victim selection
................

LruGetVictim first checks whether any buffers are still unused (not
yet on the list).  During warmup, this avoids unnecessary eviction.

If all buffers are on the list, it acquires lru_lock and walks from
the head (LRU end) looking for the first buffer with refcount == 0.
Worst case is O(n) if many buffers are pinned, but typical case is
O(1) since the LRU end is least likely to be in active use.

If the buffer header is locked (BM_LOCKED), the spinlock is
temporarily released while waiting for the header to become unlocked,
then reacquired before continuing the scan.


C. Concurrency
--------------

A single spinlock (lru_lock) protects all list mutations:

    On hit:    acquire lru_lock, remove + insert, release.
    On evict:  acquire lru_lock, remove, release.
    On new tag: acquire lru_lock, insert, release.
    Get victim: acquire lru_lock, walk list, release.

The miss path does NOT acquire lru_lock.  It only increments the
stat_misses atomic counter via PoolStatIncrement.

This is the same scalability bottleneck as ARC and LIRS (single
spinlock on every hit), but LRU operations under the lock are simpler:
just a list remove and a list insert, with no ghost list manipulation,
no target size adjustment, and no frequency counter updates.

The stat counters (stat_hits, stat_misses, stat_evictions) use
pg_atomic_uint64 and are updated via PoolStatIncrement, which batches
increments in a thread-local counter and periodically flushes to the
shared atomic.  This avoids contention on the atomic counters under
high access rates.


D. No VACUUM Integration
-------------------------

The hint_vacuum callback in the vtable is set to NULL.

When VACUUM loads a page, it goes through the normal on_new_tag path
and is inserted at the MRU end, just like any other page.  There is no
special handling to minimize cache pollution from VACUUM scans.

This is a deliberate design choice: LRU is intended as a simple
baseline.  The consequence is that a VACUUM of a large table will push
recently-used pages toward the LRU end and potentially evict them.
This is one form of the sequential scan pollution problem.


E. Performance Characteristics
------------------------------

Strengths:

  - Simplest implementation of any replacement algorithm.
  - Lowest memory overhead per buffer (~12 bytes).
  - Predictable, well-understood behavior.
  - O(1) hit and eviction operations (list manipulation).
  - Lock-free miss path.

Weaknesses:

  - No scan resistance.  A single sequential scan of a table larger
    than the buffer pool will flush every cached page, destroying the
    working set.

  - No adaptation.  LRU cannot distinguish between pages accessed once
    and pages accessed thousands of times.  It treats all accesses
    identically based solely on recency.

  - Spinlock on every hit limits scalability on high-core-count
    systems.

  - No prefetch integration.

  - Belady's anomaly applies: for certain access patterns, increasing
    the cache size can paradoxically increase the miss rate.  This is a
    classical property of LRU (and FIFO) that does not apply to stack
    algorithms like OPT.

Primary use case:

  LRU serves as a benchmarking baseline.  By running a workload on
  both LRU and an adaptive algorithm (ARC, LIRS, CAR), you can
  measure exactly how much value the adaptive algorithm provides.
  If LRU and ARC perform similarly, the workload has a simple recency
  pattern and does not benefit from adaptation.  If ARC significantly
  outperforms LRU, the workload has scan or frequency patterns that
  adaptation can exploit.


F. Trickle Writer
-----------------

The trickle writer uses an iterator to walk the LRU list from the
head (LRU end) and yield dirty, unpinned buffers for background
writing.

LruTrickleIter:

    int  current;     -- current position in the LRU list
    int  remaining;   -- candidates left to return

LruTrickleIterBegin allocates the iterator using:

    MemoryContextAlloc(TopMemoryContext, sizeof(LruTrickleIter))

This uses TopMemoryContext (not palloc) because trickle writers run
in a long-lived backend context.  The standard palloc would allocate
in CurrentMemoryContext, which may be a short-lived transaction or
statement context that gets reset before the iterator is freed.

LruTrickleIterNext walks from the current position, acquiring and
releasing lru_lock to read each node's next pointer.  It returns the
global buffer ID of each dirty+unpinned buffer it finds.

LruTrickleIterEnd frees the iterator with pfree.


G. Stat Counter Batching
-------------------------

Three stat counters are maintained:

    stat_hits       (slot 0) -- incremented on every cache hit
    stat_misses     (slot 1) -- incremented on every cache miss
    stat_evictions  (slot 2) -- incremented on every eviction

All three use PoolStatIncrement, which accumulates increments in a
per-backend local counter (lru_local_stats[]) and periodically flushes
to the shared pg_atomic_uint64.  This reduces contention on the shared
atomic counters.

The pg_stat_lru view reads the shared atomic counters directly via
pg_atomic_read_u64.  The values may lag slightly behind real-time due
to batching, but are eventually consistent.


H. References
-------------

LRU is a classical algorithm with no single originating paper.  It has
been standard in operating systems research since the 1960s.

The key reference for understanding LRU limitations:

    L.A. Belady, "A study of replacement algorithms for a virtual-
    storage computer", IBM Systems Journal, Vol. 5, No. 2, 1966.

This paper introduced Belady's anomaly and the optimal replacement
algorithm (OPT/MIN), and established the framework for analyzing
page replacement algorithms that is still used today.

Standard textbooks covering LRU include:

    A.S. Tanenbaum, "Modern Operating Systems" (any edition),
    Chapter on Memory Management.

    A. Silberschatz, P.B. Galvin, G. Gagne, "Operating System
    Concepts" (any edition), Chapter on Virtual Memory.


I. Known Limitations
---------------------

1. No scan resistance.  A single sequential scan of a table larger
   than the cache will evict every cached page.  This is the most
   significant practical limitation.

2. No adaptation.  LRU cannot learn from workload patterns.  It
   cannot distinguish between a page accessed once in a scan and a
   page that is part of the hot working set.

3. Spinlock on every hit.  The lru_lock spinlock is acquired on every
   cache hit to move the page to the MRU end.  This limits
   scalability under high concurrency.

4. No prefetch integration.  The prefetch_hint callback is NULL.
   LRU cannot use prefetch information to improve eviction decisions.

5. No VACUUM awareness.  VACUUM pages pollute the cache by being
   inserted at the MRU end like any other page.

6. Belady's anomaly.  For certain access patterns, increasing the
   cache size can increase the miss rate.  This is inherent to LRU
   and cannot be fixed without changing the algorithm.

7. This algorithm is provided purely as a baseline for comparison
   with adaptive algorithms.  It is not recommended for production
   workloads where scan resistance or adaptation is needed.
